</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose FideDiff, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Deblurring Foundation model ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We introduce our foundation model in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Deblurring Foundation model ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and the Kernel ControlNet with kernel estimation and timestep prediction in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Kernel ControlNet ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. At last, we clarify the whole training pipeline and the loss definition in Sec.  <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Training Pipeline ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Deblurring Foundation model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, FLUX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs, 2024</a>)</cite>, etc, the image restoration field has witnessed significant progress from a generative perspective. However, ensuring the rationality and effectiveness of few/one-step models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, while maintaining fidelity, remains an urgent issue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">According to the analysis in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Forward and Backward Reformulation for Deblurring ‣ 3 Task Formulation and Data Preparation ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we perform consistency training to enforce that the model produces temporally consistent estimates of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for noisy latents sampled along the same trajectory, thereby enabling single-step inference. Given a training pair (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{LQ},I_{HQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{LQ},I_{HQ}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=g(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=g(n)</annotation></semantics></math>), we first utilize the Variational AutoEncoder (VAE) to encode <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{LQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{LQ}</annotation></semantics></math> with the downsampling factor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and gain the latent representation <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{LQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{LQ}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>). Then, the latent diffusion model takes <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as input to predict <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\epsilon}=\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(z_{t},t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\epsilon}=\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(z_{t},t,c)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable text embedding. As defined in Eq. <a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3 Forward and Backward Reformulation for Deblurring ‣ 3 Task Formulation and Data Preparation ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we obtain the predicted <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and decode it as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}_{HQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}_{HQ}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will clarify the timestep <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}</annotation></semantics></math> used in the inference phase in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Kernel ControlNet ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To further pursue the fidelity, we abandon the various distillation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that are intended for more natural content generation, and adopt a GAN discriminator <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sauer et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sauer et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to ensure that the generated samples closely match the real data distribution. Given the restored <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{HQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{HQ}</annotation></semantics></math> (encoded by VAE from <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{HQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{HQ}</annotation></semantics></math>), the discriminator is responsible for distinguishing between the real high-quality representation <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{HQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{HQ}</annotation></semantics></math> and the generated <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, providing feedback to the generator to refine the restoration process and enhance the fidelity of the reconstructed data. The GAN discriminator consists of a pretrained UNet encoder (initialized from DMs’ encoder) and several convolution blocks to generate true or false.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.01641v1/pipelinev2.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/187;" width="494" height="187" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Training pipeline of our FideDiff. Optimized <math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is used for inference.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Kernel ControlNet</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">End-to-end learning often overlooks crucial motion information, and incorporating kernel priors into DMs remains underexplored. For instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024b)</a></cite> employs two vision encoders to extract semantic information as a plugin, whereas the vanilla ControlNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> accepts conditions like human pose, depth for generation, but has not explored kernel information. Recently,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite> adopts a two-stage reconstruction approach and proposes IRControlNet as a post-processing modifier, enhancing the quality of the repaired image. The foundation model alone is far from sufficient for high-fidelity deblurring tasks. In Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Forward and Backward Reformulation for Deblurring ‣ 3 Task Formulation and Data Preparation ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with estimated <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the network <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(z_{t},t,c,k_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\epsilon}_{\theta}(z_{t},t,c,k_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is expected to be more powerful to predict the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> given the fact <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}=k_{t}*z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}=k_{t}*z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, we design Kernel ControlNet, aiming to inject kernel information as an additional condition into the Unet of the DMs. Specifically, given a blurry input <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{LQ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{LQ}</annotation></semantics></math>, we process the blurry image with a convolutional Unet <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the image space, resulting in a blur kernel representation <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}=\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ}),k_{t}\in\mathcal{R}^{m\times m\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}=\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ}),k_{t}\in\mathcal{R}^{m\times m\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> represents the predefined kernel size. Then, we do not adopt the direct mapping used in the original ControlNet where the condition is mapped to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{in}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{in}</annotation></semantics></math> and added to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{in_{1}}=\text{Conv}(z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{in_{1}}=\text{Conv}(z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, because the kernel map, unlike depth or pose, does not have a direct spatial correspondence with the target image. Instead, we apply a filter-like module <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{in_{2}}=\text{Conv}(z_{in_{1}}),\quad W=\text{Conv}(\text{Cat}(k_{in},z_{in_{2}}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Conv</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>Cat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{in_{2}}=\text{Conv}(z_{in_{1}}),\quad W=\text{Conv}(\text{Cat}(k_{in},z_{in_{2}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O=W*z_{in_{2}},\quad z_{out}=z_{in_{1}}+Z(O)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O=W*z_{in_{2}},\quad z_{out}=z_{in_{1}}+Z(O),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a convolution initialized with zeros. Then, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{out}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{out}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the input for the subsequent ControlNet <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. The structure of the ControlNet remains the same as the original DM’s encoder, and the initial parameters of ControlNet are copied from those of the encoder in DMs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">t-prediction.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, Kernel ControlNet plays another crucial role in estimating the timestep <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> during the inference phase. As discussed in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Forward and Backward Reformulation for Deblurring ‣ 3 Task Formulation and Data Preparation ‣ FideDiff: Efficient Diffusion Model for High-Fidelity Image Motion Deblurring" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, consistency training enables one-step sampling during inference. However, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> remains unknown at this stage. To address this, we design a small regression model <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that follows the kernel estimation network <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to get the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}=\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}=\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ}))</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the more complex and prolonged the kernel trajectory, the higher the blur level, and consequently, the larger the timestep <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> becomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Training Pipeline</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our training has three stages. The first stage is the foundation model training:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\textit{EA-LPIPS}}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{G}(\hat{I}_{HQ})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EA-LPIPS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\textit{EA-LPIPS}}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{G}(\hat{I}_{HQ}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{G}=-\mathbb{E}_{t,I_{LQ}}(\log\mathcal{D}(\hat{z}_{HQ}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{G}=-\mathbb{E}_{t,I_{LQ}}(\log\mathcal{D}(\hat{z}_{HQ})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{D}=-\mathbb{E}_{t,I_{HQ}}(\log\mathcal{D}(z_{HQ}))-\mathbb{E}_{I_{LQ}}(\log(1-\mathcal{D}(\hat{z}_{HQ})))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{D}=-\mathbb{E}_{t,I_{HQ}}(\log\mathcal{D}(z_{HQ}))-\mathbb{E}_{I_{LQ}}(\log(1-\mathcal{D}(\hat{z}_{HQ}))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The second stage is the kernel estimation network <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>’s pretraining. We define the reblur loss as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{reblur}=\mathcal{L}_{1}(\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ})*I_{HQ},I_{LQ})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{reblur}=\mathcal{L}_{1}(\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ})*I_{HQ},I_{LQ}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which uses the reblurred image to regulate the kernel estimation via back propagation. For the third stage, the foundation model is frozen, and only the Kernel ControlNet {<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F,C,M,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F,C,M,T}</annotation></semantics></math> } is optimized:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\textit{EA-LPIPS}}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{reblur}+\lambda_{4}\mathcal{L}_{time}(t,\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ})))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EA-LPIPS</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{1}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\textit{EA-LPIPS}}(\hat{I}_{HQ},I_{HQ})+\lambda_{3}\mathcal{L}_{reblur}+\lambda_{4}\mathcal{L}_{time}(t,\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{M}(I_{HQ}))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">EA-LPIPS is the LPIPS loss with an edge detection model, proven useful in many works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{time}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{time}</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regression loss, detailed in the supplementary.</p>
</div>
